Dependency injection in C# is basically: **don’t make your class go out and build its own tools**—have the tools handed to it from the outside.[5][1] That keeps classes more **modular**, easier to **test**, and less tied to specific implementations.[5][2]

Think of it like this:

- A class is a *made guy* that needs certain gear to do the job.
- Instead of the class saying, “I’ll go fetch my own hammer,” it says, “Bring me a hammer.”
- Some other part of the app hands it the hammer at runtime.[2][5]

In C#, DI usually works like this:

- You define an **interface** for what the class needs.[1][5]
- You create one or more **implementations** of that interface.[1]
- You pass the dependency into the class, most commonly through the **constructor**.[1][2][5]
- A DI container like `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection` can create and supply the right objects for you.[1][5]

Example:

```csharp
public interface IMessageWriter
{
    void Write(string message);
}

public class ConsoleMessageWriter : IMessageWriter
{
    public void Write(string message) => Console.WriteLine(message);
}

public class Worker
{
    private readonly IMessageWriter _writer;

    public Worker(IMessageWriter writer)
    {
        _writer = writer;
    }

    public void DoWork()
    {
        _writer.Write("Work completed");
    }
}
```

Here, `Worker` doesn’t care *which* writer it gets—just that it gets one.[5][2] That means you can swap `ConsoleMessageWriter` for a fake writer in tests without changing `Worker`.[5][4]

The most common style is **constructor injection**, where the dependency is required up front and passed in when the object is created.[2][5] Other styles exist too, like **property/setter injection**, where the dependency is set after construction.[1][3]

